    24	1. A ballot is not a stored message. The voter grants in one action, and only while both directions of the link are up. The action reads the candidate's journal and the candidate's current epoch at that moment. Taking the same grant again changes nothing. A lost ballot is a grant that is never taken. A delayed ballot is a grant taken later. Reordering is the order of grant actions. A reply to the candidate that would carry an older epoch is outside this representation, because there is no buffered request.
    25	
    26	2. An extend is applied to the follower inside the coordinator's send. The follower leaves one reply. A later send replaces an untaken reply, and the replaced reply is lost. Two extends to the same follower are not in flight together, and a reply is not delivered twice. Delay of an extend, while the link is up, is the send not having been taken yet. A send on a down link is dropped and not queued.
    27	
    28	3. A reply carries three fields the message list does not name: success or failure, the match index, and the sender's epoch. A reply whose epoch is higher than the coordinator's makes the coordinator a follower and clears `votedFor`. It is not also applied as a success or a failure. A reply at an equal or lower epoch is applied. Success sets `matchIndex` to the last index the request carried and `nextIndex` to one past that. Failure decreases `nextIndex`. `nextIndex` stays at least 1, so `prevIndex` does not go below 0.
    29	
    30	4. The durable vote and the grant are one action. A grant has no observable event of its own. An `Elected` cause names each counting voter by `(proc, seq)` where `seq` is that member's latest observable record at the grant, or 0 when the member has none. The candidate's own vote uses the same rule, with the sequence number of the candidate's latest record when the election begins. Causes are ordered by member name.
    31	
