   455	3.6.1    Election restriction
   456	
   457	In any leader-based consensus algorithm, the leader must eventually store all of the committed
   458	log entries. In some consensus algorithms, such as Viewstamped Replication [66], a leader can be
   459	elected even if it doesn’t initially contain all of the committed entries. These algorithms contain
   460	additional mechanisms to identify the missing entries and transmit them to the new leader, either
   461	during the election process or shortly afterwards. Unfortunately, this results in considerable addi-
   462	tional mechanism and complexity. Raft uses a simpler approach where it guarantees that all the
   463	committed entries from previous terms are present on each new leader from the moment of its elec-
   464	tion, without the need to transfer those entries to the leader. This means that log entries only flow in
   465	one direction, from leaders to followers, and leaders never overwrite existing entries in their logs.
   466	     Raft uses the voting process to prevent a candidate from winning an election unless its log con-
   467	tains all committed entries. A candidate must contact a majority of the cluster in order to be elected,
   468	which means that every committed entry must be present in at least one of those servers. If the can-
   469	didate’s log is at least as up-to-date as any other log in that majority (where “up-to-date” is defined
   470	precisely below), then it will hold all the committed entries. The RequestVote RPC implements this
   471	restriction: the RPC includes information about the candidate’s log, and the voter denies its vote if
   472	its own log is more up-to-date than that of the candidate.
   473	     Raft determines which of two logs is more up-to-date by comparing the index and term of the
   474	last entries in the logs. If the logs have last entries with different terms, then the log with the later
