   492	3.6.2    Committing entries from previous terms
   493	
   494	As described in Section 3.5, a leader knows that an entry from its current term is committed once
   495	that entry is stored on a majority of the servers. If a leader crashes before committing an entry,
   496	future leaders will attempt to finish replicating the entry. However, a leader cannot immediately
   497	conclude that an entry from a previous term is committed once it is stored on a majority of servers.
   498	Figure 3.7 illustrates a situation where an old log entry is stored on a majority of servers, yet can
   499	still be overwritten by a future leader.
   500	     To eliminate problems like the one in Figure 3.7, Raft never commits log entries from previ-
   501	ous terms by counting replicas. Only log entries from the leader’s current term are committed by
   502	counting replicas; once an entry from the current term has been committed in this way, then all prior
   503	entries are committed indirectly because of the Log Matching Property. There are some situations
   504	where a leader could safely conclude that an older log entry is committed (for example, if that entry
   505	is stored on every server), but Raft takes a more conservative approach for simplicity.
   506	     Raft incurs this extra complexity in the commitment rules because log entries retain their orig-
   507	inal term numbers when a leader replicates entries from previous terms. In other consensus algo-
   508	rithms, if a new leader re-replicates entries from prior “terms”, it must do so with its new “term
   509	number”. Raft’s approach makes it easier to reason about log entries, since they maintain the same
   510	term number over time and across logs. In addition, new leaders in Raft send fewer log entries from
   511	previous terms than in other algorithms, since other algorithms must send redundant log entries to
   512	renumber them before they can be committed; however, this may not be very important in practice,
   513	since leader changes should be rare.
