11:## Choices
15:1. **Empty AppendEntries and commitIndex.** Figure 3.1 step 5 sets `commitIndex` to `min(leaderCommit, index of last new entry)`. A heartbeat carries no entry. The prose in §3.5 says heartbeats carry `leaderCommit` so followers learn what is committed. For an empty request that passed the consistency check, the index used is `prevLogIndex`, not the follower's log length. Extra entries the follower has past `prevLogIndex` are not committed by a heartbeat.
17:2. **The leader counts toward a majority.** Figure 3.1 initialises `matchIndex` to 0 for every server. The prose in §3.5 says an entry is committed once the leader that created it has replicated it on a majority of the servers, and the leader holds the entry. `matchIndex[self]` is the leader's log length. A majority is more than half of the caller's latest configuration, and the leader is included only when that configuration contains the leader.
25:6. **Who is sent RPCs.** RequestVote and AppendEntries go to every other server in the sender's latest configuration. During AddServer catch-up the leader also sends AppendEntries to the server being added.
29:8. **Catch-up.** §4.2 is not part of the build. Figure 4.1 step 2 plus the assembler's note are implemented as: catch-up is complete when the new server's log is identical to the leader's, and it is complete in the same step when the logs already match. Until then the leader replicates with ordinary AppendEntries. A nondeterministic timeout (the election timeout) may answer `timeout` instead. There is no fixed round count.
31:9. **One change at a time.** A second request that arrives at a member with a change still in progress is answered `busy`. A request to add a member already present, to remove a member who is absent, or to remove the last member is answered `rejected` and appends nothing. Otherwise the leader waits until the previous configuration is committed (the initial configuration counts as committed), appends one new configuration entry, and answers `ok` once that entry's index is at most `commitIndex`. The pending request is volatile and is discarded on crash or on stepping down, in which case the answer is `not_leader` if the member is still up.
45:16. **Committed epoch.** `Committed.epoch` is the member's `currentTerm` when its `commitIndex` first covers that index. For the leader, that is the term in which the entry is marked committed, including entries from older terms that commit indirectly. J1.5(a) uses that same epoch: a later coordinator, elected in a strictly higher epoch, must hold the entry at election. Using the entry's own term would reject a correct indirect commit, because a leader elected between the entry's term and the commit need not have held an entry that was not yet committed.
53:20. **A command at a non-leader is ignored.** The step is still taken (§ scenario format).
61:24. **Bounds used for every scenario run.** A first attempt with `MaxTerm = 3`, `MaxMsgs = 12` and unordered delivery was still growing at depth 12: 2,856,132 distinct states and 2,071,413 queued. It would not finish under the 24G cap, so the cap was not raised. The runs below use the tighter bounds. S1–S8 each still have a completing path, and TLC exhausted each of those state spaces.
64:    - `MaxLog = 3`. S8 appends three configuration entries and no shared scenario appends more.
65:    - `MaxMsgs = 4`. One candidate's three RequestVote messages fit. A second candidate cannot queue its own three beside those, and a new leader cannot queue heartbeats until a slot is free.
69:    - At most two servers have role candidate or leader. Two is enough for a second leader in one term, and for a stale leader beside one new candidate.
73:    - AppendEntries goes to the lowest-ranked peer that is behind on `matchIndex` and has none queued. When no peer is behind, the leader sends one heartbeat to the lowest peer, and only while it has no AppendEntries queued.
74:    - A higher term resets `nextIndex` and `matchIndex`. Both are read only while the server is leader, and `BecomeLeader` overwrites them.
76:25. **Bounds used only by `--explore`.** Initial configuration `{m1, m2, m3, m4}`, and the same `MaxTerm`, `MaxLog`, `MaxMsgs`, delivery order, send order, campaign cap and `m5` rule as choice 24. One command (`c1`) is enabled once a leader exists and the network is empty. Crashes, configuration changes, cuts, drops and redeliveries are capped at 0.
78:    The free environment did not finish. With crashes, cuts, drops and three membership changes enabled, depth 8 held 1,431,483 distinct states and the queue was still growing. Allowing those actions only when the network was empty and nobody was a candidate, and only one crash, one change and one cut, was still growing at depth 43 (2,621,185 distinct, 442,704 queued). Explore therefore checks elections and a single command (8,325 distinct states). Each failure-model shape in S1–S8 is checked by that scenario's own run.
92:| S1 | PASS | One command, crash, new election, restart. Leader completeness and one leader per term. |
93:| S2 | PASS | Isolated leader is stale; the other three are a majority of four and elect in a new term. |
97:| S6 | PASS | A new election can still finish after the 2–2 cut because a RequestVote that was sent while the link was up may be delivered afterwards (choice 12). The new leader's term is new, so J1.1 holds, and the committed command is on a majority. |
99:| S8 | PASS | Three successive one-server changes. J1.3 holds by the append rule. |
100:| S1 mutation | FAIL J1.1 | A candidate becomes leader with one vote (its own). Two servers can both do that in one term. |
102:| S3 mutation | FAIL J1.1 | A follower that appends a configuration entry becomes leader in its current term, which is the leader's term. |
107:| S8 mutation | FAIL J1.3 | A membership change appends the previous configuration unchanged. |
123:| S8 | PASS | PASS |
131:| S8 mutation | FAIL J1.3 | FAIL J1.3 |
138:The S1 mutation was aimed at J1.1: a candidate becomes leader on its own vote, so two servers can both do that in one term. With `MaxMsgs = 4` the second `StartElection` cannot queue three RequestVote messages beside the first candidate's three, and `BecomeLeader` cannot queue its heartbeats until a slot is free. Delivering the first candidate's requests makes the others vote in that term, so a later election uses the next term. The violation TLC reaches is J1.5. In the trace, `m1` commits command `c1` in term 1, then `m4` becomes leader in term 2 on its own vote with an empty log. That breaks leader completeness. The two-leader state in one term was not reached under this message cap.
148:Explore does not crash, cut, drop, or change membership. S1–S8 are the runs that do.
150:A third concurrent candidate or leader is outside the campaign cap. J1.1 fails when a second leader appears in a term, so a third is not required to witness that clause.
154:Self-removal is implemented: once the removal entry commits, that server becomes a follower and stays up. No shared scenario removes the coordinator, and explore's remove skips the leader.
