    24	- Five independent Go goroutines own their member state. A discrete-event scheduler delivers one input at a time and waits for its response; this chooses an interleaving of concurrent actors without using wall time. Network requests and responses travel independently through a controlled queue. No goroutine reads another member's state.
    25	- Logical timer units have no physical duration. Election timeouts are seeded random values in [40,80], heartbeats every 10, ordinary delivery delays in [1,3]. Seeded occasional extra delays, loss, and duplicates sample G1.2 as well as explicitly injected link failures. Stable member iteration order and scheduler tie-breaking make an entire run reproducible.
    26	- A scheduler event is one timer firing, message delivery, administrator action, or fault. Bounds and settling count these events, including events that emit no contract record. Auxiliary `Scheduled` records expose this count in the observe file. Contract records emitted by one transition are atomic and are all checked; a bound never splits a transition. Actions fire after a seeded delay of 0 to 2 logical units. The next step is armed immediately before the action, so its synchronous records can match.
    27	- The durable store is a member-owned in-memory storage object separate from volatile protocol state. Crash/restart preserves exactly term, vote, and journal. This simulates the stated crash model, not an OS process kill or disk corruption. Observer sequence numbers survive restart as observer metadata. Commit index and administrator requests do not survive. Configuration is recovered by scanning the durable log.
    28	- All members begin with the scenario's initial configuration and an empty journal. A member whose latest configuration excludes itself does not initiate elections, but it still processes and votes on incoming RPCs without membership filtering. A removed leader finishes its pending configuration reply and steps down after commitment. No removed-server disruption suppression from omitted section 4.2 is added.
    29	- Leaders replicate to their latest configuration, plus any server currently being caught up. On election all nextIndex values start at last index + 1 and matchIndex at zero; the leader counts its own durable journal in quorums. Responses carry the corresponding request's matched index; stale responses never decrease matchIndex or increase nextIndex beyond evidence. Failed probes decrement nextIndex with a floor at matchIndex + 1.
