1:# Model of Specification F
9:## Choices
21:5. `Elect` is the election timeout. It is enabled for every up follower and candidate whose epoch is below `MaxEpoch`. An extend or a grant does not disable it. A coordinator does not take it. There is no timer variable. Once `matchIndex` has caught the log and `nextIndex` is past the end, the empty extend is not sent: it would change no stored state. The specification's periodic heartbeat has no clock to hang on here.
37:13. A command at a member who is down, who is not coordinator, or whose journal is already at `MaxLogLen`, is taken and appends nothing. It emits no `ChangeRequested`. Every scenario action and every explore fault emits `Fault` from `proc` `env`. That record counts in the event bound. `cut` and `drop` include the resolved directed links.
51:## Concerns
65:## Predictions
93:S6, unmutated, does not finish for a protocol reason. After the commit, the cut separates `{m1, m2}` from `{m3, m4}` in a configuration of four. A majority is three. Neither pair can collect three grants. `m5` is not in the configuration: a ballot is sent only to the candidate's configuration, and `m5`'s grant would not count. `m5` also cannot win that configuration. The coordinator never extends a member outside the configuration, so `m5`'s journal is still empty, and a member who holds the committed command will not grant. The second `Elected` does not occur. `MaxEpoch` is not what stops it. The same cut with a higher epoch cap still has no second election.
97:## Bounds
103:| MaxEpoch | 3 | A completing path of S1 and of S2 is two elections, epochs 1 and 2. Epoch 3 is one further campaign. `Elect` is enabled only while `epoch < 3`, so epoch 3 is reachable and epoch 4 is not. An election-only skeleton at this cap was about two million distinct states and fit well under the heap. |
104:| MaxLogLen | 4 | S8 appends three configuration entries on a completing path. The fourth slot is spare. S4's completing path is one command and one configuration entry. |
105:| MaxRounds | 3 | Catch-up of an empty journal over a short log is one successful extend. Two further rounds cover a failure reply and a retry. |
111:`--explore` uses the same epoch, log, and round caps, with no scenario steps. Its fault counters are `MaxCrashes = 1`, `MaxAdmin = 3`, `MaxLinkFaults = 4`, `Commands = {c1}`, and an event cap of 400. No shared scenario crashes a member more than once or issues more than three admin requests (S8). S6's cut is four undirected pairs, and explore fails one undirected pair per action, so four is the smallest counter that still contains that cut. The event cap matches the shared scenarios' `bound.events`, which is enough for a short completing path. Grants and other actions that emit no observable event are bounded by the epoch and log caps, not by the event cap.
115:### Amendment
117:A run of S1 informed this, not a verdict. With `Elect` enabled for every follower and candidate under `MaxEpoch` 3, TLC had already printed `SCENARIO_DONE` and was still growing: at diameter 22 it had 27,655,462 distinct states and 13,410,381 queued, about 4.8 million new distinct states a minute. The prefix at diameter 13, before the command and the replies, was 45,364 distinct states. That run was stopped. It is not a result.
125:`MaxEpoch` stays 3. The predictions are unchanged.
